---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/base-layout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About — Documenter"
  description="Why Documenter exists: a privacy-first way to fit photos, signatures and PDFs to the strict file-size rules of Indian exam and government forms."
>
  <main id="main-content" class="container section">
    <div class="prose">
      <h1>About Documenter</h1>
      <p>
        Documenter started with a small, maddening problem. You fill in an online application for an
        exam or a government service, reach the upload step, and the portal refuses your photo
        because it is 62KB instead of "20KB to 50KB", or because it is 400 pixels wide instead of the
        exact size demanded. So you hunt for a converter, upload your photo to some website you have
        never heard of, and hope for the best.
      </p>
      <p>
        That last part bothered us. Handing your photograph, signature or identity documents to an
        anonymous server just to shrink a file is a poor trade. Modern browsers are perfectly capable
        of resizing and compressing files on their own — so that is exactly what Documenter does.
      </p>

      <h2>What it is</h2>
      <p>
        Documenter is a small toolkit for fitting documents to a required size and shape:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Resize a photo or signature to an exact KB limit and pixel dimensions.</li>
        <li>Compress a PDF under a size cap — keeping text selectable, or flattening scans.</li>
        <li>Combine images into a single PDF.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Everything runs in your browser. Nothing is uploaded. You can read more on the
        <a href="/privacy">privacy page</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Where it's going</h2>
      <p>
        This is an early version. More formats and more tools will follow, and every change will keep
        the core rule intact: your files stay on your device.
      </p>
    </div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
